#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286" :question/problem "![](images/00/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319" :question/problem "![](images/38/38ab4af726729b5c9804f4aa13801d637e5e62eda085d6611174586827ef8c11.png)\n\nConsider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2,$ and $C_3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179600 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3124c0f4-4c6e-55ec-9349-8032b33b382f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415737} {:answer/id #uuid "990826b9-b6bc-588a-80e6-36af02d8f2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415750} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e72ea2-4855-5111-a3dd-cd04fb8b2403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415752} {:answer/id #uuid "e80ae482-54b0-51f8-b405-74f535b48c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415759} {:answer/id #uuid "f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415762} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4c61a4-7435-599c-afcf-0efe6b1cbdb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a47f5f1d-1328-52e8-8c90-bfca32946e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-125419" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44901697-c22c-53ba-bb65-14f1081533bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12z\\,j-4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415738} {:answer/id #uuid "49a222b7-ced0-5334-a476-927827cacddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-z)x\\,i+2{z}^{3}\\,j-2{y}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415740} {:answer/id #uuid "6829492d-3f92-5206-8c0f-7015db0fd5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415743} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e04aa2-786d-5004-9af7-6898c9098f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}∇(6{z}^{2}-z-2y)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415755} {:answer/id #uuid "ecda143d-5ef3-59db-8104-b35b72d657b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∇(6{z}^{2}-z-2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6829492d-3f92-5206-8c0f-7015db0fd5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415743} :answer-field/id #uuid "f968e9bb-1698-556c-94c3-47e9ed5d0dfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fc12b03-7934-57bc-b6a6-b1ef9ff5ade7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125425" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z$ is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P=y-z,Q=6{z}^{2},R=-4y.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}((y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F=(y-z)\\,i+6{z}^{2}\\,j-4y\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248f19de-9914-5bab-a8d3-9da16c44f98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415736} {:answer/id #uuid "46aa7018-0782-59bf-8f3c-c481140a5532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415739} {:answer/id #uuid "d3414a07-8bc9-5a0b-8216-6eb6ba5bc40b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415754} {:answer/id #uuid "e26dd6a1-e2c4-5310-904f-4261a100d96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415757} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ecc9b3-f68e-5629-8e61-d1c693d7d7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46aa7018-0782-59bf-8f3c-c481140a5532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415739} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4c4c60-58a2-5309-91fd-f009939a8cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6da1b103-36df-5031-b2ef-46cb571416a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-77134" :question/problem "![](images/74/746a05b2345e50173ed898b741944ba435d17a44dfc075848be9d8fcd2f2ad90.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $PQRP,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={-{C}_{1}}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ is the same path as ${C}_{1}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f5be3d-e9a5-5b71-9237-641c57905868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415742} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4582a3-9b84-5136-bbc8-4a5b81e6b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415744} {:answer/id #uuid "8d876fc2-4110-59e7-a123-efe4ad754f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415748} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd91543-0a88-52f5-89ca-61af0000467f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415751} {:answer/id #uuid "ba144e5b-e62a-51ae-8288-ad68474c5bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fd91543-0a88-52f5-89ca-61af0000467f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415751} :answer-field/id #uuid "16807e0b-25b3-557d-839e-c6e7045de982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ab286f6-317f-53cd-ac02-bf3eb20fe604" :question/math-academy-id "q-77148" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c3b1312e7368d8fc760a274a49280b10d4571a06ee6237b28fc8a8b1b7316d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $DEFD,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {-{C}_{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{3}$ is the same path as ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55fc80b9-6193-508c-b227-e04b2840c9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{51}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415741} {:answer/id #uuid "7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{112}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415745} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ded463-bc16-568f-8c0b-62a6554bc47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415756} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cf1e9e-5de9-575b-be80-c45bb969b3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415758} {:answer/id #uuid "ff980988-6413-52f6-84a6-6d1daa6d7016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7527daea-f713-54e2-9b27-ffd3fc3bb110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{112}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415745} :answer-field/id #uuid "b523b1e1-5cbd-5efb-b1af-a137b9b5cd25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8399ab15-70cf-5db8-b1d1-61088de736be" :question/math-academy-id "q-77244" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4ce481ac8babb7a3e5707f1ef44417b12e73abe4b4c2143c19b45332642c6d2.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment  from the point $B(0,8)$ to the point $A(-2,8),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from the point $A(-2,8)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from $A$ to $O.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $-2\\le t\\le 0.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-{t}^{3}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =xy\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{3})j \\\\  & =i-3{t}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-{t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (i-3{t}^{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{t}^{4}+3{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16+{[-\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{6}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =16+[0-(-\\frac{(-{2}^{5})}{5}+\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{2})] \\\\  & =16-\\frac{192}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{112}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "126d13a7-71fa-5051-a4a2-28f29e56cf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415734} {:answer/id #uuid "2216ca5d-6c6a-5107-a068-323114e3fc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415735} {:answer/id #uuid "7aae6b46-36e9-538a-8e22-b5381b749d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415746} {:answer/id #uuid "7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415747} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea13c33-b0aa-51a8-a9cd-a56c81fb144a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e44c518-6f87-55b7-bbc1-4434ed19cb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415747} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adcd9af-5265-5496-ada0-3911ecad9cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3412530f-81b3-51bb-95a9-4599a55eb207" :question/math-academy-id "q-77250" :question/problem "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nLet ${C}_{2}$ be the section of parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from $A$ to $B.$ Then,\n $-{C}_{2}$ will be the same path but travelled from $B$ to $A.$ The path $-{C}_{2}$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+(4-{t}^{2})\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=4-{t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =x\\,i=t\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i-2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i)\\cdot (i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
